  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA decaying MCS struggled to move eastward out of central Nebraska \nthis morning. A few raindrops made it to the surface, however most \nof the forecast area remained dry. By noon, temperatures had \nreached the mid 70s under cloudy skies. Highs this afternoon are \nexpected to top out near climate normals, in the low to mid 80s. \n\nA weak cold front draped from south-central Nebraska through \nnorthwest Iowa could become the focus point for redevelopment of \nconvection, as a shortwave moves across the Central Plains this \nafternoon. Current trends in the majority of the CAMs keep storm \ndevelopment well to our west, over western Nebraska. However, a few \nstill want to initiate storms along the boundary near northeast \nNebraska late this afternoon, and bring them southeast across \neastern Nebraska this evening. While the first scenario appears more \nlikely, any storms that do manage to develop in our area would be \nfueled by 1000-2500 J/kg of instability with 30-40kts of shear \nsustaining updrafts, making them capable of producing large hail and \ndamaging winds. \n\nLow pressure will settle southeast into the Great Lakes, drawing the \ncold front across the region and bringing temperatures down into the \nlow 80s for Wednesday and Thursday. The cooler temperatures will be \naccompanied by pleasant, dry weather as surface high pressure \nsettles in to the Plains. \n\nWarmer temperatures return as an upper ridge drifts into the central \nCONUS, Friday. Highs will return to the upper 80s and low 90s by the \nweekend and remain there through early next week. A handful of ridge-\nriding shortwaves will bring periodic chances for showers and \nstorms.",
